# CHAPTER 1 · PRECISION MOTIONS

Fast vim is not fast typing. It is putting the cursor exactly where
the edit happens in one or two keys. Every motion you learn here also
becomes the target of an operator in chapter 2, so it pays off twice.

## 1.1 IN-LINE SNIPING: f t F T ; ,

`f{char}`{normal} jumps ON the next occurrence of {char} in the line.
`t{char}`{normal} stops one column BEFORE it ("till").
`F{char}`{normal} and `T{char}`{normal} do the same backwards.
`;`{normal} repeats the last f/t/F/T forwards, `,`{normal} backwards.

ATTENTION: your config maps `;`{normal} to `:`{normal} (open command
line), which shadows repeat-forward. `,`{normal} still repeats
backwards. If you start missing `;`{normal} badly — and you will once
f/t is in your fingers — remove the `;` mapping in lua/mappings.lua.

Warm up on this line — `fv`{normal}, `fo`{normal}, `t;`{normal},
`Fd`{normal}, `,`{normal}:

stream_valid <= stream_ready and (not fifo_full) and crc_ok;

EXERCISE 1: The word "found" appears twice below. Land on the second
one with `2ff`{normal} (second match of f — both hits are the f of
"found"), then delete that copy with `daw`{normal}:

The compiler found found an error in the design.

EXERCISE 2: Delete the second argument INCLUDING the comma. Cursor to
the comma with `f,`{normal}, then delete till the closing paren with
`dt)`{normal}:

update(sensor_id, calibration_data)

EXERCISE 3: The line below ends with the wrong character. `$`{normal}
jumps to end of line, `r`{normal} replaces one character. Fix it with
`$r;`{normal} — three keys, no insert mode:

signal counter : integer range 0 to 255]

## 1.2 word VS WORD: w b e VS W B E

Lowercase `w`{normal} `b`{normal} `e`{normal} treat punctuation as
word boundaries. Uppercase `W`{normal} `B`{normal} `E`{normal} only
break on whitespace — perfect for paths, URLs and code identifiers.
`ge`{normal} / `gE`{normal} jump to end of the PREVIOUS word.

Compare `w`{normal} vs `W`{normal} hops on this line:

self.config["timeout"] = os.environ.get("TIMEOUT", 30)

EXERCISE 4: Delete the entire first path below in ONE motion. A WORD
includes the equals sign and slashes: `daW`{normal} from anywhere
inside it:

path=/usr/share/nvim/runtime backup_path=/tmp/old_config

## 1.3 COUNTS MULTIPLY EVERYTHING

Any motion takes a count: `3w`{normal}, `2f,`{normal}, `5j`{normal}.
With `:set rnu`{vim} the line numbers ARE the counts for `j`{normal}
and `k`{normal} — read, type, land.

EXERCISE 5: With the cursor at the start of the line below, delete
the first three words with one command: `d3w`{normal}:

one two three four five six

## 1.4 STRUCTURE JUMPS: { } % H M L gg G

`{`{normal} / `}`{normal} jump by paragraph (blank-line blocks).
`%`{normal} jumps between matching ( ) [ ] { } — and sits at the core
of "how do I get to the other end of this thing".
`H`{normal} `M`{normal} `L`{normal} jump to top/middle/bottom of the
visible screen. `gg`{normal} / `G`{normal} take counts: `42G`{normal}
goes to line 42.

EXERCISE 6: Delete the inner parenthesised group below. Put the
cursor on its opening paren with `2f(`{normal}, then `d%`{normal}
deletes from there to its matching paren:

if (mask and (flags or enable)) else fallback

## 1.5 SCROLL WITHOUT MOVING, MOVE WITHOUT SCROLLING

`zz`{normal} centers the view on the cursor — use it constantly.
`zt`{normal} / `zb`{normal} push the cursor line to top/bottom.
`<C-d>`{normal} / `<C-u>`{normal} scroll half pages (cursor follows).
`<C-e>`{normal} / `<C-y>`{normal} scroll one line (cursor stays).

Drill: `<C-d>`{normal} `<C-u>`{normal} `zz`{normal} until automatic.

## 1.6 SEARCH IS A MOTION

`/text`{normal} is not just for finding — it is the universal
teleport, and it composes with operators: `d/end`{normal} deletes up
to the next "end". `*`{normal} searches the word under the cursor
forwards, `#`{normal} backwards; `n`{normal} / `N`{normal} repeat.
This becomes lethal in chapter 4 with `gn`{normal}.

TIP: `<Esc>`{normal} is mapped in your config to clear search
highlighting (`:noh`{vim}) — hammer it freely after searching.

## WORKOUT

Redo exercises 1-6 top to bottom without reading the instructions.
Goal: under 60 seconds, all six ✓.

Key reading: [motion.txt](motion.txt), [word-motions](word-motions),
[scroll-cursor](scroll-cursor)

